# Language and literature, stages 3 to 5 (ages 13 to adult).
# Revise a sentence, compare interpretations, weigh evidence for a reading.
# Passages marked as examples are written for Primer; quoted lines of
# Shakespeare are in the public domain.

@ lang.3.literature
goal: Read a passage closely: separate what it says from how it says it, and support an interpretation with evidence from the text.
intro: Close reading asks not only what a text says but how it makes its meaning: through word choice, imagery, voice and form. An interpretation is a claim, and the words on the page are its evidence.
  show: table Lens | Question to ask ;; theme | what idea is being explored? ;; symbol | what stands for more than itself? ;; voice | who speaks, and in what tone? ;; form | how does the shape carry the meaning?
demo: "The orchard had been her father's. Now the apples fell unpicked, and wasps worked the soft fruit." Nothing here says that she grieves. Yet "had been", "unpicked" and the wasps doing the only work left in the orchard build a picture of loss and neglect.
  show: chips had been | unpicked | wasps worked
M: Match each term to its meaning.
  pairs: Theme => An idea the work explores | Symbol => A thing that stands for more than itself | Voice => Who speaks, and in what manner | Form => The shape and structure of the work | Tone => The attitude the writing takes
  hint: Theme is about ideas; form is about shape.
  why: These are the working terms of close reading. Each names a different way in which a text carries meaning.
P: In the orchard passage, what does the unpicked fruit most plausibly suggest?
  + Neglect that follows a loss :: The father is gone and nobody has taken up his work.
  - Only that the apples ripened late :: [literal-only] That reads the detail as a fact about fruit and ignores "had been her father's".
  - That the narrator dislikes apples :: [unsupported-inference] Nothing in the passage is about taste.
  hint: Read the detail beside the sentence before it.
  why: A detail gains meaning from its neighbours. Placed after "had been her father's", the unpicked apples point to his absence.
C: The two sentences report the same event. What does the change of verb do?
  left: text She walked home.
  right: text She trudged home.
  + The second adds weariness or reluctance without changing what happened :: Same journey, different feeling.
  - The second describes a different event :: [denotation-confusion] In both she goes home on foot.
  - The second is simply more formal :: [register-confusion] "Trudged" is no more formal. It carries a different feeling.
  hint: What extra information does "trudged" carry?
  why: Words with the same basic meaning differ in connotation. Writers choose among them to control tone.
>P: "He folded the letter along its old creases and put it back in the drawer with the others." Which reading does the wording best support?
  + He has reread this letter many times, and keeps others like it :: "Old creases" and "with the others" both say so.
  - He is a tidy person, and nothing more :: [literal-only] That leaves "old creases" and "the others" unexplained.
  - He means to destroy the letter :: [unsupported-inference] He puts it back; nothing suggests destruction.
  - The letter is unimportant to him :: [reversed-reading] A letter folded and refolded and kept has mattered.
  hint: Why would the creases be old?
  why: Every claim about a text should point to words on the page. Here two small phrases carry the whole reading.
done: You read for how, not only for what. Take the same habit to a poem or a scene, and go on to Shakespeare's verse.

@ lang.3.shakespeare
goal: Read Shakespeare's language with confidence: paraphrase early modern English, hear iambic pentameter and tell comedy from tragedy by its shape.
intro: Shakespeare wrote about four hundred years ago in early modern English. Most of his words are still ours, but some have changed their meaning, and much of his writing is verse with a steady beat.
  show: table Then | Now ;; thou art | you are ;; wherefore | why ;; hath | has ;; anon | soon ;; ere | before
demo: "But, soft! what light through yonder window breaks?" The line has ten syllables in five pairs, each a light beat followed by a strong one: but SOFT, what LIGHT, through YON, der WIN, dow BREAKS. This is iambic pentameter.
  show: chips but SOFT | what LIGHT | through YON | der WIN | dow BREAKS
M: Match each early modern word to its meaning today.
  pairs: wherefore => why | thou => you (to one person) | hath => has | anon => soon | ere => before
  hint: "Wherefore" is the one most often misread.
  why: A handful of words account for much of the strangeness. Learn them and the lines open up.
P: Juliet says, "O Romeo, Romeo! wherefore art thou Romeo?" What is she asking?
  + Why must you be Romeo, a Montague? :: "Wherefore" means why. His name is the obstacle.
  - Where are you, Romeo? :: [false-friend] "Wherefore" looks like "where" but means "why".
  - Who are you, Romeo? :: [guessed-from-context] She knows exactly who he is. That is the problem.
  hint: Check "wherefore" in the table.
  why: She is lamenting that the man she loves belongs to her family's enemies.
F: Count the beats.
  frame: A line of iambic pentameter has __ strong beats
  answer: 5 | five
  x 10 :: [counted-syllables] Ten is the number of syllables. They come in five pairs, one strong beat to a pair.
  hint: "Penta" means five.
  why: Pentameter means five measures. Each measure, an iamb, is a light syllable followed by a strong one.
G: Comedy or tragedy? Sort the features.
  bin Comedy: ends in marriages | disguises and mistaken identities are untangled | order is restored with laughter
  bin Tragedy: ends in the hero's death | a fatal error or flaw drives the plot | order is restored at great cost
  hint: Think of how each kind of play ends.
  why: In Shakespeare's theatre the names describe a shape. Comedies move through confusion to weddings; tragedies move through error to death.
>P: Queen Gertrude says, "The lady doth protest too much, methinks." In Shakespeare's English, "protest" meant to vow or declare. What does the line mean?
  + I think the lady makes so many vows that they are hard to believe :: She over-insists.
  - I think the lady objects too strongly :: [modern-sense] That is today's meaning of "protest", not Shakespeare's.
  - I think the lady should speak up more :: [reversed-reading] "Too much" says that she speaks too much already.
  hint: Put "vow" in place of "protest".
  why: Familiar words can be false friends across four centuries. Reading Shakespeare means checking what a word meant then.
done: Paraphrase first, then listen for the beat. Next, read a whole scene aloud.

@ lang.3.world-lit
goal: Place major works of world literature in their cultures and periods, and compare how different traditions treat a shared theme.
intro: Literature is older and wider than any one language. Reading beyond your own tradition shows which human concerns are shared, and how differently they can be told.
  show: table Work | Tradition ;; The Epic of Gilgamesh | ancient Mesopotamia ;; The Mahabharata | ancient India ;; The Odyssey | ancient Greece ;; The Tale of Genji | Heian Japan ;; Journey to the West | Ming China ;; Things Fall Apart | modern Nigeria
demo: In the Epic of Gilgamesh a king, shaken by his friend's death, travels to the edge of the world to find a way never to die. He fails, and comes home to the walls of his city. One of the oldest stories we have is already about accepting mortality.
  show: flow a friend dies > the king seeks endless life > he fails > he returns to his city
M: Match each work to its tradition.
  pairs: The Epic of Gilgamesh => Ancient Mesopotamia | The Tale of Genji => Heian Japan | The Mahabharata => Ancient India | Things Fall Apart => Twentieth-century Nigeria | Journey to the West => Ming China
  hint: Genji is a court romance; Gilgamesh was written on clay tablets.
  why: Knowing where and when a work comes from is the first step in reading it on its own terms.
O: Put the works in order, from the oldest to the most recent.
  items: The Epic of Gilgamesh | The Odyssey | The Tale of Genji | Don Quixote | Things Fall Apart
  ends: Oldest | Most recent
  hint: Clay tablets, then Greek oral epic, then an eleventh-century court, then 1605, then 1958.
  why: Gilgamesh goes back some four thousand years, the Odyssey nearly three thousand, Genji one thousand, Don Quixote four hundred, and Things Fall Apart appeared in 1958.
C: Two translators render the same line of a poem. What should a reader conclude?
  left: text Word for word, keeping the original order, in plain prose.
  right: text Freely reworded to keep the rhyme and rhythm.
  + Each translator has chosen what to preserve: sense, sound or form :: No version keeps everything.
  - One of them must simply be wrong :: [single-correct-translation] Both can be faithful, to different things.
  - The freer one is not a translation at all :: [literalism] Keeping a poem's music is also a kind of fidelity.
  hint: Can a translation keep the exact words and the exact sound at once?
  why: Translation involves trade-offs. Reading world literature well means knowing that you read through a translator's choices.
>P: Odysseus in the Greek Odyssey and Monkey in the Chinese Journey to the West both undergo long journeys full of trials. What does comparing them most usefully show?
  + The journey as a test that changes the traveller appears in both traditions, though each prizes a different kind of wisdom :: Shared shape, different values.
  - One story must have copied the other :: [influence-assumed] Similar patterns arise independently; there is no evidence of contact here.
  - The two are really the same story :: [flattening] Homecoming to a household and a pilgrimage for scriptures are different goals.
  hint: Look for what is shared and for what differs.
  why: Comparison is most useful when it holds likeness and difference together.
done: Read one work from a tradition new to you, and ask what it assumes that your own tradition does not.

@ lang.3.rhetoric
goal: Identify appeals to character, emotion and reason (ethos, pathos, logos), and notice when persuasion stands in for evidence.
intro: Aristotle described three ways in which a speaker persuades. Ethos rests on the speaker's character and credibility, pathos on the audience's feelings, and logos on evidence and reasoning.
  show: table Appeal | Rests on | Sounds like ;; ethos | the speaker's credibility | "In twenty years as a nurse…" ;; pathos | the audience's feelings | "Imagine your own child…" ;; logos | evidence and reasoning | "Accidents fell by a third…"
demo: A campaign for a lower speed limit says: "I have driven ambulances on this road for ten years. Last year it saw fourteen crashes; the road beside it, with the lower limit, saw four. How many more families must get that phone call?" One sentence builds trust, one gives evidence, one stirs feeling.
  show: steps Ethos: ten years driving ambulances here ;; Logos: fourteen crashes against four ;; Pathos: families and the phone call
G: Sort each line by its main appeal.
  bin Ethos: As a nurse of twenty years, I have seen this at first hand. | Our findings were checked by an independent panel.
  bin Pathos: Imagine your own child waiting in that queue. | Do not let their sacrifice be forgotten.
  bin Logos: Accidents fell by a third after the limit was lowered. | The bridge carries ten tonnes and the lorry weighs twelve, so it cannot cross.
  hint: Is the line about who is speaking, how you feel, or what follows from the facts?
  why: Ethos asks you to trust the speaker, pathos to feel, and logos to follow a chain of evidence.
E: Revise the claim so that it rests on evidence rather than on a loaded word.
  tokens: This policy | is a disaster{has cost twice its budget,is an outrage} | .
  limit: 1
  aim: Change one phrase so that a reader could check the claim.
  accept: 2=has cost twice its budget
  x 2=is an outrage :: [swapped-one-loaded-word-for-another] "Outrage" is as uncheckable as "disaster". It tells the reader what to feel, not what happened.
  hint: Which version could be confirmed or refuted by looking at the accounts?
  why: "Has cost twice its budget" is a claim that can be checked. Loaded words ask for agreement without offering grounds.
P: "You cannot trust her plan for the park. She does not even live here." What is wrong with this as an argument?
  + It attacks the person and leaves the plan unexamined :: Where she lives does not show whether the plan is good.
  - It relies too much on statistics :: [misidentified-the-appeal] It offers no statistics at all.
  - Nothing: where someone lives decides whether their plan is sound :: [accepted-the-fallacy] A plan stands or falls on its merits.
  hint: Does the objection say anything about the plan itself?
  why: Attacking the speaker in place of the argument is called ad hominem. It is ethos turned into a weapon.
>P: A resident says: "Four of the five street lamps on this road are broken, so walking here at night is less safe than it should be." Which appeal carries the argument?
  + Logos :: A fact, and a conclusion drawn from it.
  - Ethos :: [credibility-confusion] The speaker says nothing about being trustworthy or expert.
  - Pathos :: [topic-not-method] Safety is an emotive subject, but the method here is evidence and inference.
  hint: Look at how the sentence is built: a fact, then "so".
  why: An appeal is identified by how it persuades, not by its subject. A count followed by "so" is reasoning from evidence.
done: Name the appeal, then ask what evidence stands behind it. Use the same test on the next advertisement you see.

@ lang.3.essays
goal: Build an argumentative essay: a thesis that could be disputed, reasons with evidence, an opposing view fairly stated, and a conclusion that follows.
intro: An essay argues for one claim, its thesis. A good thesis is something a reasonable person could disagree with, and the rest of the essay earns it.
  show: flow thesis > reasons with evidence > the opposing view, answered > conclusion
demo: "School starts early" is a fact, and "This essay is about school hours" is an announcement. A thesis takes a position and gives a reason: "Secondary schools should start after nine, because teenagers' sleep runs later and rested pupils learn more."
  show: table Sentence | What it is ;; School starts early. | a fact ;; This essay is about school hours. | an announcement ;; Schools should start after nine, because… | a thesis
P: Which sentence is a thesis?
  + Cities should turn one car lane on each main road into a bus lane. :: It takes a position that can be argued for and against.
  - This essay will discuss buses. :: [announcement-not-claim] It says what the essay is about, not what it claims.
  - A bus is a vehicle that carries passengers. :: [fact-not-claim] Nobody would dispute it, so there is nothing to argue.
  - Are buses a good idea? :: [question-not-claim] A question raises the issue; a thesis answers it.
  hint: Which one could a reasonable person disagree with?
  why: A thesis is a claim that needs defending. Facts, questions and announcements do not.
O: Put the parts of an argumentative essay in order.
  items: The thesis, stating the claim | The first reason, with evidence | The second reason, with evidence | The strongest opposing view, and an answer to it | A conclusion that follows from the reasons
  ends: Opens the essay | Closes it
  hint: The claim comes before its support; the reply to opponents comes before the close.
  why: A reader should know the claim first, see it supported, see it tested against its best objection, and then be shown what follows.
C: Two ways of presenting the other side. Which serves the essay better?
  left: text Opponents of later starts just want children to be lazy.
  right: text Opponents point out that later starts push sport and part-time work into the evening.
  + The right: it states the opposing view fairly, so answering it strengthens the essay :: A real objection, honestly put.
  - The left: it makes the opponents look foolish :: [straw-man] Defeating a view nobody holds proves nothing.
  - Neither: an essay should never mention the other side :: [one-sided-is-stronger] Ignoring the best objection leaves the reader to raise it unanswered.
  hint: Which version would an opponent recognise as their own view?
  why: An honest essay meets the strongest form of the opposing case. Misrepresenting it is the straw-man fallacy.
>P: A draft's thesis reads: "Social media has good and bad sides." What is the most useful revision?
  + Take a position: "Schools should teach pupils how to check what they see on social media, because false stories spread faster than corrections." :: Now there is a claim and a reason.
  - Add emphasis: "Social media has very good and very bad sides." :: [style-over-claim] Stronger adjectives do not make a position.
  - List more sides: "Social media has good, bad, social, political and economic sides." :: [no-position] A longer list still commits to nothing.
  hint: Could anyone disagree with the draft as it stands?
  why: A thesis that nobody could dispute gives the essay nothing to prove. Revision begins by committing to a claim.
done: Claim, support, objection, conclusion. Draft a thesis of your own and test whether someone could disagree with it.

@ lang.3.linguistics-intro
goal: Take language apart into its layers: sounds (phonemes), meaningful parts (morphemes) and sentence structure (syntax).
intro: Linguistics studies language as a natural system. It works in layers: the sounds that distinguish words, the smallest parts that carry meaning, and the rules that arrange words into sentences.
  show: table Layer | Unit | Example ;; phonology | phoneme | the first sounds of pat and bat ;; morphology | morpheme | un + break + able ;; syntax | phrase | the old dog
demo: "Pat" and "bat" differ in a single sound, and that one sound changes the meaning: the two form a minimal pair. "Unbreakable" has three morphemes: un (not), break, and able (can be).
  show: chips un | break | able
F: Count the morphemes.
  frame: The word "unhappiness" has __ morphemes
  answer: 3 | three
  x 4 :: [counted-syllables] Un-hap-pi-ness has four syllables. Morphemes are parts with meaning: un, happy, ness.
  x 1 :: [whole-word] The word can be split into smaller meaningful parts.
  hint: Split it into pieces that each mean something.
  why: Un (not), happy and ness (state of being) are three morphemes. Syllables measure sound; morphemes measure meaning.
G: Is it a minimal pair?
  bin Minimal pair: pat / bat | ship / sheep | fan / van
  bin Not a minimal pair: cat / dog | stop / spot | run / running
  note stop / spot :: The same sounds in a different order differ in more than one position.
  hint: A minimal pair differs in exactly one sound, in the same position.
  why: Minimal pairs prove that two sounds are separate phonemes in a language: swapping one for the other changes the word.
P: "The dog bit the man" and "The man bit the dog" use exactly the same words. What changes the meaning?
  + Syntax: the order of the words shows who does what :: In English, position marks subject and object.
  - Morphology: the words have different endings :: [wrong-level] No word has changed its form.
  - Phonology: the words are pronounced differently :: [wrong-level] Each word sounds just as before.
  hint: Nothing about the individual words has changed.
  why: English relies on word order to mark who acts and who is acted upon. That is a fact about syntax.
B: Build a word meaning "not able to be read" from its morphemes.
  bank: un | read | able | re | ing | ness
  answer: un | read | able
  x re :: [wrong-morpheme] "Re" means again. The meaning asked for is "not".
  x ness :: [wrong-morpheme] "Ness" makes a noun naming a state, as in "sadness".
  hint: Start with the part that means "not".
  why: Un + read + able: each morpheme contributes one piece of the meaning.
>P: A small child says, "I goed to the park." What does the error reveal?
  + The child has learned the rule for the past tense and applied it to an irregular verb :: Nobody taught the child "goed".
  - The child is copying what adults say :: [imitation-only] Adults say "went". The child has never heard "goed".
  - The child is producing sounds at random :: [noise] The error is systematic: it follows the regular pattern exactly.
  hint: Where could "goed" have come from, if nobody says it?
  why: Errors like "goed" show that children build rules rather than only imitating. Language is a rule-governed system.
done: Sounds, parts, structure. Try splitting five long words into their morphemes.

@ lang.3.media
goal: Separate report from opinion, notice how framing and word choice steer a reader, and check a claim against its source.
intro: News reaches you already shaped: someone chose the facts, the words and the picture. Reading critically means asking what happened, who says so, and what has been left out.
  show: flow what is claimed? > who says so? > what is the evidence? > what is left out?
demo: Two headlines report one decision: "Council slashes library hours" and "Council adjusts library hours to protect budget". The fact is the same: opening hours fall from 50 to 38 a week. The verbs tell you how to feel about it.
  show: table Headline | Verb | Effect ;; Council slashes library hours | slashes | sounds like damage ;; Council adjusts library hours | adjusts | sounds like care
C: Compare the two headlines about the library.
  left: text Council slashes library hours
  right: text Council adjusts library hours to protect budget
  + They report the same fact with different framing :: The choice of verb carries a judgement.
  - They report two different decisions :: [framing-as-fact] The decision is the same: hours fall from 50 to 38.
  - The second is neutral because it is calmer :: [calm-equals-neutral] "Adjusts" and "to protect" also steer the reader, in the other direction.
  hint: What actually happened to the opening hours in each?
  why: Framing works through word choice. A calm tone is not the same as neutrality.
G: Statement of fact, or opinion?
  bin Can be checked: The council voted 7 to 4. | Opening hours will fall from 50 to 38 a week. | The library lent 90,000 books last year.
  bin Opinion: The decision is a disgrace. | Libraries matter more than car parks. | The council has its priorities right.
  hint: Could you look it up and find it true or false?
  why: A factual claim can be checked against records. An opinion is a judgement: it can be argued for, but not looked up.
K: A post shows brown water pouring from a tap, with the caption "Our city's water, this morning". Turn over as few clues as you need to decide whether the photograph supports the caption.
  clue Likes: The post has forty thousand likes.
  clue Image search: The same photograph appears in a news report from another country, dated 2014.
  clue Account: The account that posted it was created last month.
  clue Comments: Many commenters say that they believe it.
  need: 2
  + The photograph does not support the caption :: It was taken elsewhere, years ago.
  - The photograph supports the caption :: [popularity-as-evidence] Likes and comments show that people reacted, not that the picture is what it claims to be.
  hint: Which clue tells you where the photograph came from?
  why: The origin of the image settles it. Popularity, a new account and trusting comments tell you nothing about where a picture was taken.
>P: A headline reads "Study shows chocolate improves memory". The study tested twelve people for one day and was paid for by a chocolate maker. What is the best response?
  + Treat it as weak evidence: a tiny sample, a short test and an interested funder :: Not worthless, but far from settled.
  - Accept it, because it is a scientific study :: [authority-word] The word "study" does not tell you how strong the evidence is.
  - Conclude that the opposite must be true :: [overcorrection] Weak evidence for a claim is not evidence against it.
  hint: How many people, for how long, and who paid?
  why: Critical reading weighs the evidence. Sample size, duration and funding all bear on how far to trust a result.
done: Ask who says so and how they know. Try it on two reports of the same event.

@ lang.3.second-language
goal: Use the habits that make a new language stick: notice patterns and cognates, practise a little every day, and beware of false friends.
intro: Learning a second language is less about memorising lists than about meeting the language often and noticing how it works. Many words will look familiar, because languages share ancestors and borrow from one another.
  show: table English | Spanish | French ;; family | familia | famille ;; important | importante | important ;; music | música | musique
demo: Spanish "el gato negro" means "the black cat", but word for word it says "the cat black". In Spanish the adjective usually follows its noun. Learn the pattern and you can build phrases you have never heard.
  show: chips el: the | gato: cat | negro: black
M: Match each Spanish word to its English meaning.
  pairs: gato => cat | perro => dog | casa => house | libro => book | agua => water
  hint: "Libro" is related to the English word "library".
  why: A small stock of common nouns, met often, is the foundation that everything else builds on.
P: "El gato negro" is "the black cat". "Casa" (house) is a feminine noun, taking "la", and "white" is "blanca". How do you say "the white house"?
  + la casa blanca :: Article, noun, then adjective.
  - la blanca casa :: [english-word-order] That keeps English order. In Spanish the adjective usually follows the noun.
  - el casa blanca :: [wrong-gender-article] "Casa" is feminine, so it takes "la".
  hint: Copy the pattern of "el gato negro", with the feminine article.
  why: A pattern learned from one phrase transfers to another: article, noun, adjective, each agreeing in gender.
G: Which habits help a new language to stick?
  bin Helps: ten minutes every day | saying sentences aloud | reviewing a word just before you would forget it
  bin Helps little: three hours once a month | reading rules without ever using them | translating every word one at a time
  hint: Little and often, and in use.
  why: Short daily practice, speaking aloud and well-timed review build lasting memory. Rare long sessions and rules without use do not.
>P: In Paris you see a sign reading "Librairie". The French for a lending library is "bibliothèque". What should you expect behind the door?
  + A bookshop :: "Librairie" is a false friend: it looks like "library" but means bookshop.
  - A lending library :: [false-friend-trap] That is "bibliothèque". "Librairie" only looks like the English word.
  - A bakery :: [guessed] The word is related to "livre", book, not to bread.
  hint: The question tells you which word means library.
  why: False friends look alike in two languages but differ in meaning. Cognates help, but they need checking.
done: A little every day, patterns over lists. Keep a notebook of false friends as you meet them.

@ lang.4.linguistics
goal: Analyse sentences formally: constituent structure and structural ambiguity, entailment as against implicature, and rule-governed sound patterns.
intro: Formal linguistics states precisely what speakers know without being taught. Syntax describes how words group into constituents, semantics what must be true if a sentence is true, and phonology the patterns in a language's sounds.
  show: table Field | Object | Tool ;; syntax | constituents | bracketing and trees ;; semantics | truth conditions | entailment ;; phonology | sound patterns | rules over classes of sounds
demo: "I saw the man with the telescope" has one string of words and two structures. If "with the telescope" attaches to "saw", it names the instrument. If it attaches to "the man", it describes him.
  show: pair text [saw [the man] [with the telescope]] || text [saw [the man with the telescope]]
C: The two bracketings contain the same words. How do the readings differ?
  left: text [saw [the man with the telescope]]
  right: text [saw [the man] [with the telescope]]
  + In the left, the man has the telescope; in the right, the telescope is what I saw him with :: Attachment decides the meaning.
  - They mean the same; brackets are only notation :: [structure-is-notation] Each bracketing corresponds to a different meaning that speakers can tell apart.
  - The left is ungrammatical :: [one-parse-only] Both structures are well formed, which is why the sentence is ambiguous.
  hint: In each, find what "with the telescope" is grouped with.
  why: Structural ambiguity arises when one string of words has two constituent structures. Meaning is read from the structure.
P: Which statement is entailed by "Kim killed the wasp"?
  + The wasp died :: It cannot be false if the sentence is true.
  - Kim dislikes wasps :: [implicature-not-entailment] It may be a natural guess, but the sentence can be true without it.
  - Kim was stung :: [world-knowledge] Nothing in the sentence requires it.
  hint: Which one must be true in every situation in which the sentence is true?
  why: An entailment follows necessarily. If Kim killed the wasp, the wasp died: denying it is a contradiction.
P: "Some of the students passed" suggests that not all of them did. What shows that this is an implicature and not an entailment?
  + It can be cancelled without contradiction: "Some passed; in fact, all did." :: The sentence stays coherent.
  - It is usually what the speaker means :: [frequency-as-test] Being usual does not make an inference necessary.
  - The word "some" means "not all" :: [built-into-the-word] If it did, "some, in fact all" would contradict itself, and it does not.
  hint: Try adding "in fact, all of them did".
  why: Implicatures arise from how we use sentences and can be cancelled. Entailments cannot be cancelled without contradiction.
G: Sort the plural nouns by how the ending is pronounced.
  bin Ending sounds like s: cats | books | cups
  bin Ending sounds like z: dogs | bees | pens
  bin Ending adds a syllable: buses | dishes | judges
  hint: Say each aloud, and attend to the last sound of the singular.
  why: The plural has three pronunciations, chosen by rule: an extra syllable after hissing sounds, s after other voiceless sounds, and z elsewhere. Speakers follow the rule without ever being taught it.
>P: "Flying planes can be dangerous." Why is the sentence ambiguous?
  + "Flying planes" can be planes that fly, or the act of flying them :: Two structures for one phrase.
  - "Dangerous" has two meanings :: [lexical-not-structural] The word means the same on both readings.
  - The sentence lacks punctuation :: [surface-fix] No punctuation mark distinguishes the two readings.
  hint: Ask what the subject is on each reading: some planes, or an activity?
  why: In one structure "planes" is the head, modified by "flying". In the other "flying" is the head, with "planes" as its object.
done: Structure determines meaning. Bracket three ambiguous newspaper headlines for practice.

@ lang.4.lit-theory
goal: Distinguish the major critical approaches by the question each one asks of a text, and apply several to the same work.
intro: A critical theory is a way of reading: a decision about which questions to put to a text. Different approaches notice different things in the same work.
  show: table Approach | Its central question ;; formalism | how do the text's own features make its meaning? ;; structuralism | what underlying patterns and oppositions organise it? ;; Marxist criticism | how does it relate to class and material conditions? ;; feminist criticism | how does it represent gender and power? ;; post-structuralism | where does its meaning become unstable? ;; reader-response | what does the reader do to make meaning?
demo: Take the tale of Cinderella. A formalist notices the pattern of three nights and the midnight deadline. A feminist critic notices that her only way out of servitude is to be chosen. A Marxist critic notices unpaid labour, and marriage as a leap between classes.
  show: steps Formalist: three nights, and a deadline ;; Feminist: rescue only by being chosen ;; Marxist: unpaid labour and a change of class
M: Match each approach to the question it asks.
  pairs: Formalism => How do the text's own features make meaning? | Marxist criticism => How does it relate to class and material conditions? | Feminist criticism => How does it represent gender and power? | Reader-response => What does the reader do to make meaning? | Post-structuralism => Where does its meaning become unstable?
  hint: Formalism stays inside the text; reader-response looks at the person reading.
  why: Each approach is defined by its question. Knowing the question tells you what kind of evidence it will look for.
G: Sort these remarks about Cinderella by the approach behind them.
  bin Formalist: The three nights at the ball build a pattern that the lost slipper breaks. | The midnight deadline gives the plot its tension.
  bin Feminist: The heroine's only escape is to be chosen by a man. | The tale sets women against one another as rivals.
  bin Marxist: Her unpaid work keeps the household running. | Marriage serves as a leap from one class to another.
  hint: Pattern and structure, gender and power, or labour and class?
  why: The same story yields different observations because each approach directs attention to different features.
P: Roland Barthes wrote of "the death of the author". What does the phrase claim?
  + A text's meaning is not fixed by what its author intended :: Readers and language make meaning too.
  - Only works by dead authors can be studied :: [literal-reading] The phrase is a figure of speech about authority over meaning.
  - The author's life is the key to the work :: [reversed-the-claim] That is the biographical view which the phrase rejects.
  hint: Who, on this view, has the last word on what a text means?
  why: Barthes argued against treating the author's intention as the final authority, and shifted attention to language and the reader.
>P: A critic writes of Frankenstein: "The novel sets creator against creature, light against dark, speech against silence, and then lets each pair collapse into the other." Which approach is this?
  + Post-structuralist: it shows the text's oppositions undoing themselves :: The collapse is the point.
  - Structuralist :: [stopped-at-the-oppositions] Structuralism maps the oppositions. Showing that they do not hold is the post-structuralist step.
  - Marxist :: [wrong-lens] Nothing here concerns class or material conditions.
  - Reader-response :: [wrong-lens] The critic describes the text's workings, not a reader's experience.
  hint: The critic does more than list the pairs of opposites.
  why: Deconstructive reading looks for the places where a text's organising oppositions break down.
done: A theory is a question. Read one short poem twice, through two different approaches.

@ lang.4.creative
goal: Revise creative writing: show rather than tell, choose concrete detail, keep the point of view consistent and cut what does no work.
intro: A first draft is raw material. The writing happens in revision: replacing the general with the particular, the stated with the shown, and removing every word that earns nothing.
  show: flow draft > find the vague and the stated > make it concrete > cut
demo: "She was nervous" tells the reader what to conclude. "She straightened the cutlery twice, then a third time" shows an action and lets the reader conclude it. The second version is longer by a few words and far more alive.
  show: table Telling | Showing ;; She was nervous. | She straightened the cutlery twice, then a third time. ;; The house was old. | The stairs announced every visitor.
C: Which sentence does more work, and why?
  left: text The room was messy.
  right: text Three days of cups stood along the windowsill.
  + The right: one concrete detail lets the reader see the mess and infer the neglect :: It also hints at how long it has gone on.
  - The left: it is shorter and clearer :: [summary-over-image] It is clear, but it gives the reader nothing to see.
  - They do the same work :: [detail-is-decoration] The right adds time, and a person who has stopped clearing up.
  hint: Which one could you draw?
  why: A specific image carries more than a general label, because the reader takes part in working out what it means.
E: Replace the weak verb and its adverb with one exact verb.
  tokens: He | walked slowly{shuffled,walked very slowly indeed} | across | the | hall | .
  limit: 1
  aim: Change one phrase so that a single verb does the work.
  accept: 2=shuffled
  x 2=walked very slowly indeed :: [more-words-not-better-words] Adding intensifiers makes the sentence longer, not sharper.
  hint: Which single verb already contains the slowness?
  why: "Shuffled" carries pace, manner and a hint of age or weariness in one word. An exact verb beats a general verb propped up by adverbs.
S: Select every phrase that says the same thing twice.
  + nodded her head :: What else does one nod?
  + shrugged his shoulders :: A shrug is made with the shoulders.
  + whispered quietly :: A whisper is quiet already.
  - whispered a warning :: [cut-too-much] "A warning" adds what was whispered.
  - shrugged off the coat :: [cut-too-much] "Off the coat" is a different action, not a repetition.
  hint: Would the meaning survive if the extra words were removed?
  why: Redundant words dilute a sentence. "She nodded" and "he shrugged" say everything that the longer versions say.
P: A story is narrated in the first person: "I watched Dana open the envelope. She thought of her brother's debts." What is the flaw?
  + The narrator reports Dana's thoughts, which an "I" narrator cannot know :: The point of view has slipped.
  - The sentences are too short :: [style-not-logic] Length is not the problem.
  - A first-person narrator may never describe another character :: [overcorrection] The narrator can describe what Dana does and says, and can guess at what she thinks.
  hint: How could "I" know what Dana thought?
  why: A point of view is a contract with the reader. A first-person narrator is limited to what that person could perceive or infer.
>P: A draft reads, "The storm was very scary and loud." Which revision is strongest?
  + Thunder shook the cups on their hooks. :: A concrete image, an exact verb, and no intensifier.
  - The storm was extremely, terrifyingly loud. :: [intensifier-instead-of-image] Stronger adverbs still only tell.
  - The storm, which was a weather event, was frightening. :: [explaining-not-showing] The explanation adds words and removes life.
  hint: Which version gives the reader something to see or hear?
  why: Revision replaces the label ("scary", "loud") with the evidence for it.
done: Show, be exact, cut. Take one paragraph of your own and remove every word that does no work.

@ lang.4.history-english
goal: Trace English from Old to Modern: date its main periods, recognise the layers of its vocabulary, and explain why spelling and sound have drifted apart.
intro: English has changed so much in a thousand years that its earliest form must be learned like a foreign language. Invasion, trade, printing and migration each left a layer.
  show: table Period | Dates | A landmark ;; Old English | about 450 to 1100 | Beowulf ;; Middle English | about 1100 to 1500 | Chaucer ;; Early Modern English | about 1500 to 1700 | Shakespeare ;; Modern English | 1700 onward | Johnson's Dictionary
demo: After the Norman Conquest of 1066 the rulers spoke French and the farmers English. The animals in the field kept their Old English names: cow, pig, sheep. The meat on the lord's table took French ones: beef, pork, mutton.
  show: table In the field (Old English) | At the table (French) ;; cow | beef ;; pig | pork ;; sheep | mutton
O: Put these landmarks of English in order.
  items: Beowulf is written down | Chaucer writes The Canterbury Tales | Shakespeare's plays are first performed | Johnson publishes his Dictionary | The first text message is sent
  ends: Earliest | Latest
  hint: Old English, Middle English, about 1600, 1755, 1992.
  why: The sequence runs from Old English through Middle and Early Modern to the present, across about a thousand years.
G: Sort the words by the layer of English they belong to.
  bin Old English: house | water | bread
  bin French, after 1066: beef | justice | parliament
  bin Learned Greek coinage: telescope | biology
  hint: Everyday things, the language of the rulers, or the vocabulary of science?
  why: The commonest words are Germanic, the vocabulary of law and government is largely French, and the terms of science were built from Greek and Latin roots.
P: Why does "knight" contain a k and a gh that nobody pronounces?
  + They were once pronounced; the spelling stayed while the sounds were lost :: Spelling settled as speech went on changing.
  - They were added to make the word look French :: [invented-reason] "Knight" is an Old English word, and the letters stood for real sounds.
  - English spelling has never followed pronunciation :: [no-history] It once followed it quite closely, which is why it preserves old sounds.
  hint: Spelling was fixed largely by printing, from the late 1400s.
  why: Print fixed spellings at about the time that pronunciation was shifting. Silent letters are a record of how words used to sound.
>P: English has "kingly", "royal" and "regal", all meaning much the same. What does the triple show?
  + Three layers of vocabulary: an Old English word, a French borrowing and a Latin one :: Each has a different feel.
  - A coincidence of three unrelated words :: [no-history] The three arrived by known routes at known times.
  - English had no word for a king before the Normans :: [reversed] "King" and "kingly" are the oldest of the three.
  hint: Compare the pattern of cow and beef.
  why: English often keeps a native word beside a French and a Latin one. The native word tends to feel plain, the French courtly and the Latin learned.
done: Every English sentence is a piece of archaeology. Look up the origins of ten words in a paragraph you have read today.

@ lang.4.classics
goal: Recognise the conventions of ancient epic and trace how the Iliad, the Odyssey and the Aeneid answer one another.
intro: Three poems stand beneath much of later European literature. Homer's Iliad and Odyssey were shaped by generations of oral performance in Greek. Virgil's Aeneid, written in Latin some seven centuries later, answers them both.
  show: table Epic | Poet | Language | Subject ;; Iliad | Homer | Greek | the anger of Achilles at Troy ;; Odyssey | Homer | Greek | the long return of Odysseus ;; Aeneid | Virgil | Latin | Aeneas, from fallen Troy to Italy
demo: Epics share a set of conventions. They call on a Muse, begin in the middle of the action, name their heroes with fixed epithets such as "swift-footed Achilles", and stretch comparisons into long similes.
  show: chips invocation | in medias res | epithet | epic simile | descent to the dead
M: Match each convention to an example.
  pairs: Invocation => "Sing, goddess, of the anger of Achilles" | Epithet => "swift-footed Achilles" | In medias res => The Iliad opens in the war's tenth year | Epic simile => A warrior falls like a poplar felled by a shipwright | Descent to the dead => The hero visits the underworld to learn his future
  hint: An epithet is a fixed descriptive phrase attached to a name.
  why: These conventions recur across all three poems, and later poets signalled the epic tradition by reusing them.
P: Why did oral poets rely on fixed phrases such as "rosy-fingered Dawn"?
  + Ready-made phrases that fit the metre helped a singer to compose during performance :: Formulas were building blocks.
  - Greek had very few adjectives :: [deficit-explanation] Greek is rich in adjectives. The repetition has to do with how the poems were made.
  - Later scribes repeated lines by mistake :: [written-assumption] The formulas are systematic and fit the metre, which copying errors would not.
  hint: The poems were composed and performed aloud, without writing.
  why: Studies of living oral traditions showed that such formulas let singers compose long poems in metre as they performed.
O: Put these events from the story of Troy in order.
  items: Paris carries Helen off to Troy | Achilles quarrels with Agamemnon | Achilles kills Hector | The wooden horse is brought inside the walls | Odysseus reaches home in Ithaca
  ends: First | Last
  hint: The quarrel and Hector's death fall in the war's last year; the journey home takes ten years more.
  why: The Iliad covers a few weeks near the end of the war, the fall of the city follows, and the Odyssey tells of the journey home.
>P: The first six books of the Aeneid follow a hero's wanderings by sea, and the last six a war on land. What does this design suggest?
  + Virgil answers both of Homer's epics in one poem, claiming their inheritance for Rome :: An Odyssey, then an Iliad.
  - The resemblance is an accident :: [no-design] The correspondences are too many and too exact to be chance.
  - Virgil translated Homer into Latin :: [imitation-as-translation] The Aeneid is a new poem with its own hero and its own purpose.
  hint: Which Homeric epic is about wandering, and which about war?
  why: Virgil reverses the order of the Homeric poems and joins them, giving Rome a founding epic that measures itself against Greece.
done: Read the opening lines of all three poems side by side and find the conventions in each.

@ lang.4.comp-lit
goal: Compare works across languages on a stated basis (genre, theme, influence or translation) and say what such a comparison can and cannot show.
intro: Comparative literature reads across the borders of language and nation. A comparison needs a common ground: a shared form, a shared theme, a documented line of influence, or one text in two languages.
  show: table Basis | A question it allows ;; genre | how does the sonnet change between Italian and English? ;; theme | how do two cultures write about exile? ;; influence | what did one writer take from another? ;; translation | what survives the move between languages?
demo: The Italian sonnet divides into eight lines and six, on few rhymes. English has fewer rhyming words, and English poets reshaped the form into three quatrains and a couplet, on more rhymes. The turn of thought moved to the final two lines.
  show: table Form | Rhyme scheme | Where the thought turns ;; Italian | abba abba cde cde | after line 8 ;; English | abab cdcd efef gg | at the final couplet
C: Compare the two sonnet forms.
  left: text Eight lines and six: abba abba cde cde
  right: text Three quatrains and a couplet: abab cdcd efef gg
  + The English form needs fewer words for each rhyme and moves the turn to the closing couplet :: A form adapted to its new language.
  - They are the same form under two names :: [surface-only] The rhyme schemes and the place of the turn both differ.
  - The English form is a failed copy of the Italian :: [hierarchy-assumption] It is an adaptation that produced its own effects, such as the summing-up couplet.
  hint: Count how many lines must share each rhyme sound.
  why: Forms change when they cross languages. Comparing them shows what each language made easy or hard.
G: Evidence of influence, or only a resemblance?
  bin Evidence of influence: The later poet translated the earlier work. | Her letters name the book as her model. | He quotes its lines with a few words altered.
  bin Resemblance only: Both poems mention the moon. | Both epics contain a flood. | Both novels describe unhappy marriages.
  hint: Is there a sign that one writer actually knew the other's work?
  why: Influence needs evidence of contact. Common subjects turn up everywhere without any borrowing.
P: A Japanese haiku is measured as seventeen sound-units in three phrases, and usually includes a word naming the season. What must its English translator decide?
  + What to preserve (the count, the brevity, the image or the season word), since not all of it survives :: Translation is a choice among losses.
  - Nothing: keeping seventeen syllables is the only faithful course :: [form-fetish] English syllables are longer than Japanese sound-units, so matching the count often pads the poem.
  - Nothing: poetry cannot be translated, so it should not be attempted :: [comparison-nihilism] Imperfect translation is how most readers meet most literature.
  hint: Can everything about a poem cross into another language at once?
  why: A translator's choices are themselves evidence for comparative study: they show what each language can do.
>P: A student claims that novel B was influenced by novel A, because both are about orphans. What is needed to support the claim?
  + Evidence of contact: that the author of B read A, or distinctive shared details beyond a common subject :: A shared motif is not enough.
  - Nothing more: similarity proves influence :: [resemblance-as-influence] Orphans appear in the stories of every culture.
  - Proof that no other novel has ever been about an orphan :: [impossible-standard] The claim needs positive evidence of contact, not a search of all literature.
  hint: Could two writers arrive at an orphan hero independently?
  why: A comparison may rest on likeness, but a claim of influence needs a documented route from one work to the other.
done: State the basis of a comparison before you make it. Compare two translations of a poem that you know.

@ lang.5.philology
goal: Reconstruct a text from its surviving copies: recognise scribal errors, group manuscripts by shared errors and weigh competing readings.
intro: No author's own copy of an ancient work survives. We have copies of copies, each made by hand and each with its own mistakes. Textual criticism works backwards from the copies toward what the author wrote.
  show: table Error | What happens ;; haplography | what should stand twice is written once ;; dittography | what should stand once is written twice ;; eye-skip | the eye jumps between similar words and drops what lay between ;; interpolation | a note from the margin is copied into the text
demo: Scribes copy their exemplar's mistakes and add their own. So when two manuscripts share errors that a third lacks, the two probably descend from one lost copy. Manuscripts are grouped into families by their shared errors.
  show: flow the lost original > an early copy > a lost copy with new errors > manuscripts B and C, sharing those errors
G: Name the scribal error.
  bin Dittography: "the the river" | "remembered" written as "rememembered"
  bin Haplography: "possess" written as "posess" | "philology" written as "philogy"
  bin Eye-skip: a line missing between two lines that end in the same word | a clause dropped between two uses of "and he said"
  hint: Written twice, written once, or a jump across the page?
  why: These are mechanical errors of copying. Recognising them lets an editor explain how one reading turned into another.
K: Manuscript A reads "the king sailed at dawn". Manuscripts B and C read "the king failed at dawn". Turn over as few clues as you need to decide whether B and C count as two independent witnesses against A.
  clue Omission: B and C both leave out the same line in chapter three.
  clue Material: B is written on parchment and C on paper.
  clue Library: B and C are kept in the same library today.
  clue Shared errors: B and C share twelve other errors that A does not have.
  need: 1 | 4
  + B and C descend from one copy, so together they count as a single witness :: Their agreement is inherited.
  - B and C are independent, so they outvote A by two to one :: [counting-manuscripts] Copies of one lost exemplar repeat its errors. Their number adds nothing.
  hint: What kind of fact shows that two copies had the same parent?
  why: Shared errors show common descent. Manuscripts must be weighed and not counted: a hundred copies of one faulty exemplar are worth one witness.
P: Two manuscripts differ at one word. One has a rare, difficult word; the other a common, easy one that fits the sense. Other things being equal, which is more likely to be original?
  + The difficult one: scribes tended to replace the unfamiliar with the familiar :: The easy reading explains itself as a correction.
  - The easy one: authors write clearly :: [easier-is-original] A scribe meeting a strange word is more likely to simplify it than the reverse.
  - Neither can ever be preferred :: [no-judgement] Editors reason about which change a copyist was more likely to make.
  hint: Which change would a puzzled copyist be more likely to make?
  why: The principle is called "the harder reading is the stronger". It asks which reading best explains how the other arose.
>P: Manuscript A reads: "He blessed the bread and gave it to the poor, and he blessed the wine and gave it to the poor, and departed." Manuscript B reads: "He blessed the bread and gave it to the poor, and departed." What is the most economical explanation?
  + B's scribe skipped from the first "gave it to the poor" to the second :: A classic eye-skip between identical phrases.
  - A's scribe invented the clause about the wine :: [interpolation-assumed] It is possible, but the repeated phrase gives a simple mechanical cause for omission.
  - Both readings must be original :: [no-judgement] One arose from the other, and the task is to say which.
  hint: Look for a phrase that occurs twice in the longer version.
  why: When the longer text contains a repeated phrase and the shorter lacks exactly what lay between, eye-skip is the likeliest cause.
done: Explain how each reading arose and prefer the one that explains the others. Try it on two editions of the same poem.

@ lang.5.socioling
goal: Describe variation in language without ranking it: distinguish accent, dialect and register, and explain code-switching and prestige.
intro: Everyone speaks a variety of their language, and everyone shifts style with the situation. Sociolinguistics studies how language varies with region, class, group and occasion, and what those differences signal.
  show: table Term | What varies ;; accent | pronunciation only ;; dialect | grammar and vocabulary as well ;; register | style, according to situation ;; code-switching | movement between varieties or languages
demo: In a study of New York department stores in the 1960s, the linguist William Labov asked assistants a question whose answer was "fourth floor". Staff in the most expensive store pronounced the r most often. A single sound turned out to track social position with great regularity.
  show: bars Costliest_store=62 Middle_store=51 Cheapest_store=20 unit=%_using_some_r
G: Accent, dialect or register?
  bin Accent: "bath" said with a short or a long vowel | pronouncing or dropping the r in "car"
  bin Dialect: "I might could go" for "I might be able to go" | "wee" for "small"
  bin Register: "Dear Sir or Madam" as against "Hi there" | the wording of a legal contract
  hint: Sound only, grammar and words, or the occasion?
  why: Accent concerns pronunciation, dialect adds grammar and vocabulary, and register is the style that a situation calls for.
P: In African American English, "She be working" means that she works habitually, and differs from "She working", which means right now. What does this show?
  + A rule-governed distinction of grammar that standard English does not mark in the verb :: It encodes habitual action.
  - A random mistake :: [deficit-view] The form is used consistently and with a specific meaning.
  - Slang :: [vocabulary-not-grammar] Slang is short-lived vocabulary. This is a stable feature of grammar.
  hint: The two forms mean different things, and speakers do not interchange them.
  why: Every dialect has systematic rules. Non-standard varieties make some distinctions that the standard variety lacks.
C: Compare the two sentences.
  left: text I ain't got none.
  right: text I haven't got any.
  + Both are systematic and mean the same; they differ in social prestige and not in logic :: Each follows its own variety's rule for negation.
  - The left is illogical, since two negatives make a positive :: [logic-myth] No listener understands it as a positive. Many languages, French and Spanish among them, mark negation twice as a matter of course.
  - The left has no grammar :: [deficit-view] It follows a consistent rule: negation is marked on every word that can carry it.
  hint: Does anyone ever misunderstand the left-hand sentence?
  why: The judgement that one form is "wrong" is social. Linguistically both are regular.
>P: A bilingual teenager speaks Spanish with her grandmother, English with her teachers and a mixture of both with her friends. What is the best description?
  + Skilled code-switching, guided by audience and identity :: She chooses the right variety for each listener.
  - Confusion between two languages :: [deficit-view] She keeps them apart perfectly when the listener requires it.
  - Incomplete mastery of both languages :: [semilingual-myth] Mixing among bilingual peers is a rule-governed skill that requires command of both.
  hint: Does she mix languages with everyone, or only with those who share both?
  why: Code-switching is systematic, and sensitive to who is listening. It signals membership of a bilingual community.
done: Describe before you judge. Listen for one feature of your own speech that changes with your audience.

@ lang.5.psycholing
goal: Explain how language is acquired and processed in real time, from the evidence of children's errors, sentences that mislead the reader, priming and slips of the tongue.
intro: Psycholinguistics asks what the mind does with language: how children acquire it, how listeners build meaning word by word, and how speakers plan what they say. Much of the evidence comes from moments when the system goes wrong.
  show: table Evidence | What it reveals ;; children's errors | rules being learned ;; misleading sentences | structure built word by word ;; priming | links between words in memory ;; slips of the tongue | speech planned ahead
demo: Read "The old man the boats." Most readers take "the old man" as a phrase and then stumble. The sentence makes sense only when "the old" is read as the subject and "man" as the verb. The stumble shows that we commit to a structure before a sentence ends.
  show: pair text [The old man] the boats ?? || text [The old] [man the boats]
P: "The horse raced past the barn fell." Why do readers find this grammatical sentence so hard?
  + They take "raced" as the main verb, and must rebuild the structure when "fell" arrives :: It means "the horse that was raced past the barn fell".
  - It is ungrammatical :: [mistook-difficulty-for-error] It is well formed. Compare "The horse ridden past the barn fell".
  - It is too long to hold in memory :: [length] It has only seven words. The trouble is structural.
  hint: Try inserting "that was" after "horse".
  why: Readers build structure as each word arrives and prefer the simpler analysis. When a later word rules it out, they have to reanalyse.
O: Put the stages of a child's early language in order.
  items: Cooing | Babbling | Single words | Two-word combinations | Sentences with several clauses
  ends: Earliest | Latest
  hint: Sounds come before words, and words before combinations.
  why: Children everywhere pass through the same broad sequence, at roughly two months, six months, one year, two years, and three and beyond.
G: What does each observation tell us?
  bin Rules are learned: A child says "foots". | A child calls two unfamiliar creatures "wugs".
  bin Words are linked in memory: "Nurse" is recognised faster after "doctor". | On the tip of the tongue, a word's first sound comes back before the rest.
  bin Speech is planned ahead: "A leading list" is said for "a reading list". | A sound from a later word arrives early.
  hint: A rule applied to a new case, a link between words, or evidence of looking ahead?
  why: Over-regular forms show rule learning, priming shows connections in the mental lexicon, and anticipation errors show that upcoming words are already prepared.
>P: Readers' eyes are tracked as they read "The florist sent the flowers was pleased." Reading slows sharply at "was". What does the slow-down show?
  + Readers had already built the simpler structure and had to reanalyse it at the word that ruled it out :: Parsing is incremental.
  - Readers process one word at a time, with no structure :: [no-structure] Then there would be no reason to slow at that particular word.
  - "Was" is a rare word :: [frequency] It is among the commonest words in English.
  hint: What had readers assumed about who did the sending?
  why: A slow-down at the point of disambiguation is the signature of incremental parsing: structure is built before the sentence is complete.
done: Errors are data. Collect three slips of the tongue this week and work out what each reveals.

@ lang.5.comp-ling
goal: Model language computationally: count tokens and types, estimate the probability of a word from a corpus, and say what a language model predicts and where it fails.
intro: Computational linguistics treats text as data. A corpus is a body of text; a language model assigns probabilities to sequences of words, estimated from how often they occur.
  show: table Term | Meaning ;; corpus | a collection of text used as data ;; token | each occurrence of a word ;; type | each distinct word ;; bigram | a pair of adjacent words
demo: Take a tiny corpus: "the cat sat. the cat ran. the dog sat." The word "the" occurs three times, and twice it is followed by "cat". So the estimated probability of "cat" after "the" is 2 out of 3.
  show: table Bigram | Count ;; the cat | 2 ;; the dog | 1 ;; cat sat | 1 ;; cat ran | 1 ;; dog sat | 1
F: Estimate a probability from the counts in the tiny corpus.
  frame: The probability of "dog" after "the" is __
  answer: 1/3 | 0.33 | 0.333
  x 1/2 :: [wrong-denominator] Divide by the three occurrences of "the", not by the number of different words that follow it.
  x 1 :: [count-not-probability] One is the count. Divide it by the number of times "the" occurs.
  hint: How many times does "the dog" occur, and how many times does "the" occur?
  why: The estimate is the count of "the dog" divided by the count of "the": 1 out of 3.
F: Count types, not tokens.
  frame: "the cat sat on the mat" has 6 tokens and __ types
  answer: 5 | five
  x 6 :: [types-as-tokens] "The" occurs twice but is one type.
  hint: Count each different word once.
  why: Tokens are occurrences and types are distinct words: the, cat, sat, on, mat.
P: The bigram "dog ran" never occurs in the tiny corpus. What should a model conclude?
  + Give it a small probability by smoothing: unseen is not impossible :: The corpus is only a sample.
  - Give it probability zero, because it is not English :: [absence-as-impossibility] It is perfectly good English. The corpus was just too small to contain it.
  - Give it the same probability as "the cat" :: [ignored-the-counts] Counts still carry information about what is common.
  hint: Is a sentence impossible merely because it has not been seen?
  why: Any corpus is finite, so most possible word sequences never appear in it. Smoothing reserves some probability for the unseen.
>P: A language model completes "The capital of Australia is" with "Sydney". The capital is Canberra. What best explains the error?
  + The model predicts likely continuations from patterns in text, and "Sydney" often appears near "Australia" :: Likely is not the same as true.
  - It looked the answer up in a database that was wrong :: [retrieval-assumption] A language model generates from learned patterns. It does not consult a table of facts.
  - Language models cannot handle place names :: [capability-denial] They handle them fluently, which is what makes such errors convincing.
  hint: What is the model trained to produce: true statements, or probable text?
  why: A language model estimates what is probable given its training text. Fluent output is evidence of pattern, not of truth.
done: Count, estimate, predict, and remember what prediction does not guarantee. Work out the bigram counts for a paragraph of your own.

@ lang.5.narratology
goal: Analyse narrative with exact terms: story as against discourse, order, focalisation and the reliability of the narrator.
intro: Narratology separates what happened from how it is told. The story is the sequence of events in the order in which they occurred. The discourse is the text's own arrangement of them: its order, its speed and its point of view.
  show: table Term | Meaning ;; story | the events in the order in which they happened ;; discourse | the events as the text presents them ;; analepsis | a flashback ;; prolepsis | a flash-forward ;; focalisation | whose perception filters the telling
demo: A detective novel opens with a body. The murder happened first in the story but is narrated last in the discourse, when the detective explains it. The whole pleasure of the form lies in that gap between the two orders.
  show: table Order in the story | Order in the telling ;; 1 the murder | 1 the body is found ;; 2 the body is found | 2 the questioning ;; 3 the questioning | 3 the murder, explained at last
O: Put the events in story order: the order in which they happened, not the order in which the novel tells them.
  items: The murder is committed | The body is found | The detective questions the household | The detective explains how it was done
  ends: Happened first | Happened last
  hint: The novel tells the murder last. When did it happen?
  why: Story order is chronological. The discourse rearranges it, here by withholding the first event until the end.
M: Match each term to an example.
  pairs: Analepsis => "Ten years earlier, she had made a promise." | Prolepsis => "He would regret this before the year was out." | Internal focalisation => We learn only what one character sees and thinks | Unreliable narrator => The teller's account is contradicted by details he lets slip | External narrator => A voice outside the events tells the story
  hint: Analepsis looks back; prolepsis looks ahead.
  why: Each term names one choice in how a story is told: its order, its filter, or its teller.
P: A novel is told in the third person, yet we learn only what one character perceives and thinks. How should this be described?
  + Third-person narration with internal focalisation: one voice speaks, another mind sees :: Who speaks and who sees are separate questions.
  - First-person narration :: [voice-vision-conflation] The narrator says "she", not "I". Only the perception belongs to the character.
  - Omniscient narration :: [scope-confusion] An omniscient narrator could enter any mind. Here we are held to one.
  hint: Ask two questions: who speaks, and who sees?
  why: Distinguishing voice from focalisation explains how a text can stay in the third person and still confine us to one consciousness.
>P: A narrator insists that he is perfectly calm, while mentioning that he has checked the lock eleven times and has not slept for a week. What should a reader conclude?
  + The narrator is unreliable: the details he reports undercut his description of himself :: The text invites us to read past him.
  - The author has made a mistake :: [author-error] The contradiction is patterned and deliberate. It is how the character is revealed.
  - He is calm, because the narrator says so :: [narrator-authority] A narrator's claims are evidence to be weighed like any character's.
  hint: Compare what he claims with what he reports doing.
  why: Unreliable narration works by a gap between what the narrator asserts and what the narrative shows.
done: Ask who speaks, who sees and in what order. Chart the story order and the discourse order of a film that you know well.
